# pixie netboot-pc live env: bake a BROAD driver set into the initramfs.
#
# The live env's very first action after the kernel hands off is to
# fetch its squashfs over HTTP from the kernel cmdline `fetch=` param
# (Debian live-boot). That means the target's NIC must be driven from
# INSIDE the initramfs, before pivot-root -- iPXE loaded vmlinuz+initrd
# over the firmware's network stack, but from Linux on it's the kernel
# module that matters.
#
# mkinitramfs' default is MODULES=dep: only the drivers the BUILD HOST
# needs. MODULES=most instead bakes the broad Debian-installer IN-TREE
# driver set, so an arbitrary lab box's in-tree NIC (and storage) is
# alive before the fetch -- e.g. the Intel NICs on warp / wave pull the
# squashfs fine. Costs initramfs size (~2x); acceptable for a netboot
# appliance whose whole job is booting unknown hardware.
#
# This does NOT, on its own, cover an OUT-OF-TREE NIC: `most` only pulls
# in-tree modules. The RTL8125 2.5GbE on matx (ASRock Rack) needs the
# out-of-tree DKMS r8125, which the in-tree r8169 cannot substitute for
# in the initramfs (proven: r8169-only -> matx NIC never comes up, no
# fetch, reboot-loop). That driver is force-added to the initramfs BY
# NAME in the r8125 DKMS hook (hooks/normal/0600-pixie-r8125-dkms.hook
# .chroot); `most` never pulls it. See that hook for the RTL8125 story.
# Narrow `most` to an explicit NIC list here later if size becomes a
# problem and the target NICs are known.
MODULES=most
